  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...While seas have come down, minor soundside \nflooding (1 ft or less) is still possible for coastal areas \nadjacent to tidal rivers and sounds due to already elevated \nwaters levels and high astronomical tides. The high astronomical\ntides peak on Tuesday, but seas have fallen substantially and \nwinds will be significantly weaker than the past several days. \nAt this time, no \"new flooding,\" (i.e. ocean overwash) is \nexpected, but there still may be standing water on roadways, and\nin low lying or poor drainage areas, which would still have \ntravel impacts. For areas along the Crystal Coast and areas \nadjacent to the Pamlico Sound that saw flooding yesterday, we \nexpect to see elevated water levels through Wednesday, but \nlevels are not expected to be as high as they were on Sunday. \nThis potential will continue to be monitored over the next \ncouple of days, though, in case any coastal flood headlines are \nneeded.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Upper level ridging is forecast to expand north\nand east from the Gulf Coast to the Southeast U.S. coast \nthrough the week. Warming low-level thicknesses and the eventual\nreturn of southerly flow should allow temperatures to get back \nabove normal to close out September and welcome in October. \nMoisture and humidity will gradually return as well, but it \ndoesn't look like a quick return. This plus ridging aloft should\nhelp to keep the chance of precipitation low for much of the \nwork week.",
